module bindings.python.path_sanitizer_without_root_containment;

// A path sanitizer is the boundary a caller trusts to make a path safe: the
// name says the value is sanitized, so the callers feed it raw components and
// use the result directly against a filesystem or object store. When the
// sanitizer's only transformation is a rewrite -- separators in one convention
// replaced by another, or unsafe characters swapped out -- every `..` segment
// and every absolute prefix survives it, and a backslash-to-slash rewrite
// additionally turns a Windows traversal into the POSIX form remote stores
// act on. The rewrite is not the defect; the absence of any containment is:
// nothing compares the sanitized value, or its resolved form, against the
// base directory it is supposed to stay under.
//
// The anchor is the sanitizer's declared job read from its name -- a function
// named for sanitizing paths -- together with the observable fact that it
// rewrites its input through a `replace` call. Both conjuncts are needed:
// the name alone would reach helpers that validate rather than rewrite, and
// the rewrite alone reaches the ordinary cross-platform idiom that normalises
// a URI it has just built itself. Neither conjunct names a project or a
// vendor, so the binding fires on any codebase whose path hygiene rests on a
// rewrite-only sanitizer.
//
// A containment check anywhere in the same function neutralises the flag:
// a prefix comparison (`startswith`), the pathlib raising and boolean forms
// (`relative_to`, `is_relative_to`), shared-root comparison (`commonpath`,
// `commonprefix`), canonicalization (`resolve`, `abspath`, `realpath`,
// `normpath`) and the directory-stripping `basename`. Python context call
// paths are function-confined, so a containment check written in a sibling
// function is not credited -- the fix that moves the check into the
// sanitizer itself is the shape that clears the flag, because that is where
// the guard can actually intercept the value being handed on.
//
// Residuals, stated rather than hidden: the containment vocabulary is matched
// as substrings of dotted call paths, so a receiver whose name merely
// contains one of them (`resolved_path.replace(...)`) reads as containment,
// and a hand-rolled segment walk that rejects `..` without going through one
// of these APIs is not recognised, so its flag stands. Both are the cheaper
// direction -- silence on safe code is the one that would hide a traversal.
binding pathSanitizerWithoutRootContainment {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "python"
    and node.context.functionName contains "sanitiz"
    and node.context.functionName contains "path"
    and node.context.callPath contains "replace"
    and not containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["startswith", "relative_to", "is_relative_to", "commonpath", "commonprefix", "resolve", "abspath", "realpath", "normpath", "basename"])
  emit issue code.PathSanitizerWithoutRootContainment at node
}
